":"text-primary hover:underline","children":"Atman (Soul)"}]," ","and its oneness with"," ",["$","$L1f",null,{"href":"/what-is-brahman","eventLabel":"gita_guide:theme:brahman","trackPathName":"brahman","className":"text-primary hover:underline","children":"Brahman"}],"."]}]]}]}]]}]]}]
13:["$","div",null,{"className":"bg-card border border-border/50 rounded-2xl overflow-hidden shadow-sm","children":[["$","div",null,{"className":"p-8 border-b border-border/50 bg-muted/20","children":[["$","div",null,{"className":"flex items-center gap-3","children":[["$","svg",null,{"ref":"$undefined","xmlns":"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg","width":24,"height":24,"viewBox":"0 0 24 24","fill":"none","stroke":"currentColor","strokeWidth":2,"strokeLinecap":"round","strokeLinejoin":"round","className":"lucide lucide-list w-8 h-8 text-secondary","children":[["$","path","nlz23k",{"d":"M3 12h.01"}],["$","path","1tta3j",{"d":"M3 18h.01"}],["$","path","1rqtza",{"d":"M3 6h.01"}],["$","path","1za7za",{"d":"M8 12h13"}],["$","path","1lx6n3",{"d":"M8 18h13"}],["$","path","ik3vkj",{"d":"M8 6h13"}],"$undefined"]}],["$","h2",null,{"className":"text-3xl font-display font-bold text-foreground","children":"The 18 Chapters"}]]}],["$","p",null,{"className":"text-muted-foreground mt-2","children":"A high-level summary of the journey through the Gita."}]]}],["$","div",null,{"className":"divide-y divide-border/50","children":[["$","div",null,{"className":"p-6 sm:p-8 hover:bg-muted/10 transition-colors","children":[["$","h3",null,{"className":"text-xl font-bold text-foreground mb-2","children":"First Six Chapters: Karma Yoga"}],["$","p",null,{"className":"text-muted-foreground","children":"Focuses primarily on the nature of action, the eternity of the soul (Atman), and the introduction of selfless service. Arjuna faces his grief, and Krishna explains that the body is temporary but the soul is eternal."}]]}],["$","div",null,{"className":"p-6 sm:p-8 hover:bg-muted/10 transition-colors","children":[["$","h3",null,{"className":"text-xl font-bold text-foreground mb-2","children":"Middle Six Chapters: Bhakti Yoga"}],["$","p",null,{"className":"text-muted-foreground","children":"Shifts to the grandeur of God (Ishvara). Krishna reveals his universal, magnificent form (Vishvarupa) in Chapter 11. It establishes devotion and surrender as the most direct path to liberation."}]]}],["$","div",null,{"className":"p-6 sm:p-8 hover:bg-muted/10 transition-colors","children":[["$","h3",null,{"className":"text-xl font-bold text-foreground mb-2","children":"Last Six Chapters: Jnana Yoga"}],["$","p",null,{"className":"text-muted-foreground","children":"Focuses on philosophical knowledge, describing the three Gunas (modes of nature: Sattva, Rajas, Tamas), the difference between the material world and pure consciousness, and the culmination of surrender."}]]}]]}]]}]
14:["$","div",null,{"children":[["$","div",null,{"className":"flex items-center gap-3 mb-6","children":[["$","svg",null,{"ref":"$undefined","xmlns":"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg","width":24,"height":24,"viewBox":"0 0 24 24","fill":"none","stroke":"currentColor","strokeWidth":2,"strokeLinecap":"round","strokeLinejoin":"round","className":"lucide lucide-target w-8 h-8 text-secondary","children":[["$","circle","1mglay",{"cx":"12","cy":"12","r":"10"}],["$","circle","1vlfrh",{"cx":"12","cy":"12","r":"6"}],["$","circle","1c9p78",{"cx":"12","cy":"12","r":"2"}],"$undefined"]}],["$","h2",null,{"className":"text-3xl font-display font-bold text-foreground","children":"Practical Application Today"}]]}],["$","div",null,{"className":"prose prose-lg dark:prose-invert max-w-none text-muted-foreground leading-relaxed","children":[["$","p",null,{"children":"The brilliance of the Gita is its accessibility. You don't need to be a monk to practice its teachings. It is specifically designed for the householder—the person living in the world, with responsibilities, facing difficult choices."}],["$","ul",null,{"children":[["$","li",null,{"children":[["$","strong",null,{"children":"Focus on the Effort, Not the Outcome:"}]," ","Anxiety comes from worrying about results. Focus intensely on doing your best right now."]}],["$","li",null,{"children":[["$","strong",null,{"children":"Find Your Duty:"}]," Align your career and life choices with your natural inclinations and principles."]}],["$","li",null,{"children":[["$","strong",null,{"children":"Maintain Equanimity:"}]," Treat success and failure, heat and cold, pleasure and pain with an even mind."]}]]}]]}]]}]
15:["$","div",null,{"className":"border-t border-border/50 pt-16","children":[["$","h2",null,{"className":"text-3xl font-display font-bold text-foreground mb-8 text-center","children":"Frequently Asked Questions"}],["$","div",null,{"className":"space-y-6 max-w-3xl mx-auto","children":[["$","div",null,{"className":"bg-card p-6 rounded-xl border border-border/50","children":[["$","h3",null,{"className":"text-lg font-bold text-foreground mb-2","children":"How many chapters and verses are in the Bhagavad Gita?"}],["$","p",null,{"className":"text-muted-foreground","children":"The Gita consists of 18 chapters and exactly 700 verses (shlokas). It makes up chapters 23 to 40 of the Bhishma Parva book of the Mahabharata epic."}]]}],["$","div",null,{"className":"bg-card p-6 rounded-xl border border-border/50","children":[["$","h3",null,{"className":"text-lg font-bold text-foreground mb-2","children":"What is the core message of the Bhagavad Gita?"}],["$","p",null,{"className":"text-muted-foreground","children":"The central message is to perform your righteous duty (Dharma) with discipline and dedication, without acting out of attachment to the fruits or rewards of that labor. This path of selfless action is called Karma Yoga."}]]}],["$","div",null,{"className":"bg-card p-6 rounded-xl border border-border/50","children":[["$","h3",null,{"className":"text-lg font-bold text-foreground mb-2","children":"Did the battle of Kurukshetra really happen?"}],["$","p",null,{"className":"text-muted-foreground","children":"While it is rooted in historical events of ancient India, spiritual masters emphasize that the true battlefield is psychological—it represents the inner war between our divine nature (righteousness, kindness) and our lower nature (ego, greed, anger)."}]]}]]}]]}]
